<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<timedtext format="3">
<head>
<pen id="1" b="1" fc="#FFFFFF" fo="254" bc="#140804" bo="170" et="4" ec="#000000" fs="4" sz="110"/>
<pen id="2" b="1" i="1" fc="#FFD65A" fo="254" bc="#140804" bo="190" et="4" ec="#000000" fs="4" sz="115"/>
<wp id="1" ap="7" ah="50" av="92"/>
<ws id="1" ju="2"/>
</head>
<body>
<p t="1000" d="2239" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The sixteenth century BC.</s></p>
<p t="3280" d="2080" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Babylon, the most famous city in the</s></p>
<p t="5400" d="2319" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">world, had been sacked
and left without a ruler.</s></p>
<p t="7760" d="3320" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The people who took the throne were
neither Sumerians nor Akkadians.</s></p>
<p t="11120" d="1600" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">They had come down from the</s></p>
<p t="12760" d="1800" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Zagros mountains, a people
known for their horses.</s></p>
<p t="14600" d="2840" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">They ruled Babylon for more
than four hundred years.</s></p>
<p t="17480" d="2972" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">It was the longest dynasty
in Babylonian history.</s></p>
<p t="20493" d="2107" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">They exchanged letters
with the pharaohs of Egypt,</s></p>
<p t="22640" d="3399" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">gave daughters to their kings,
and asked them for gold.</s></p>
<p t="26080" d="1880" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Their name was the Kassites.</s></p>
<p t="28000" d="2460" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In other words, the ancient
ancestors of the Kurds.</s></p>
<p t="30750" d="2219" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Welcome to the Dîrok channel.</s></p>
<p t="45920" d="4920" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The homeland of the Kassites was
the central Zagros mountains,
rising east of the Tigris River.</s></p>
<p t="50880" d="2759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Today, Kurds and Lurs live in this region.</s></p>
<p t="53680" d="4960" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites first appear in history in
the time of Samsu-iluna, son of Hammurabi,</s></p>
<p t="58680" d="1147" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">king of Babylon.</s></p>
<p t="59867" d="3052" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the Babylonian annals, one
year was named like this: the</s></p>
<p t="62960" d="2067" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">year the Kassite army was defeated.</s></p>
<p t="65067" d="3905" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">So even then, the Kassites stood
at the gates of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="69013" d="2266" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The greatest strength of the
Kassites was their horses.</s></p>
<p t="71320" d="3600" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The horses and war chariots they
raised on the plateaus of the Zagros</s></p>
<p t="74960" d="2946" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">made them one of the
fastest armies of their age.</s></p>
<p t="77947" d="3972" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Many terms for horse breeding in
Babylonian texts came from the</s></p>
<p t="81960" d="1059" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Kassite language.</s></p>
<p t="87740" d="5799" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In 1595 BC, the Hittite king Mursili the
First marched all the way to Babylon and</s></p>
<p t="93580" d="1079" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">sacked the city.</s></p>
<p t="94699" d="2280" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The dynasty founded by
Hammurabi collapsed.</s></p>
<p t="97020" d="3052" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Hittites went home, and
Babylon was left without a ruler.</s></p>
<p t="100113" d="2279" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">It was the Kassites who filled this void.</s></p>
<p t="102433" d="4307" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Kassite kings took the throne of Babylon
and ruled for about four hundred and thirty</s></p>
<p t="106780" d="559" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">years.</s></p>
<p t="107380" d="3359" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Babylonian King List counts
thirty-six Kassite kings.</s></p>
<p t="110780" d="4439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">It is the longest reign of a single
dynasty in the history of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="115259" d="2760" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites also gave the
country their own name.</s></p>
<p t="118060" d="3039" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In their time, Babylon
was known as Karduniash.</s></p>
<p t="121139" d="4440" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Some Kurdish writers see in this name
the traces of the names Kardu and Kurd.</s></p>
<p t="125620" d="1759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites did not destroy Babylon.</s></p>
<p t="127419" d="4200" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">On the contrary, they united scattered
city-states into a single country</s></p>
<p t="131660" d="3820" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and ruled southern Mesopotamia
in peace for centuries.</s></p>
<p t="135570" d="4360" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassite king Agum recorded in
his inscription that he brought
back the statue of Marduk, which</s></p>
<p t="139970" d="1986" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Hittites had carried off from Babylon.</s></p>
<p t="141997" d="2913" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In this way, he won the hearts
of the people of Babylon.</s></p>
<p t="149609" d="4400" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the fourteenth century BC, the
world was in the hands of five great</s></p>
<p t="154049" d="4120" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">powers: Egypt, the Hittites,
Mitanni, Assyria and Kassite Babylon.</s></p>
<p t="158209" d="3280" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">These kings addressed
one another as brother.</s></p>
<p t="161529" d="3920" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Amarna Letters, found in Egypt, have
preserved this diplomacy to this day.</s></p>
<p t="165489" d="1680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In a letter to the pharaoh</s></p>
<p t="167209" d="3120" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">Akhenaten, the Kassite king Burnaburiash
complains: I put the forty minas</s></p>
<p t="170369" d="2800" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of gold you sent into the furnace, and not</s></p>
<p t="173209" d="1560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">even ten minas came out.</s></p>
<p t="174809" d="3827" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassite kings formed marriage
alliances with the pharaohs of Egypt.</s></p>
<p t="178677" d="4133" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Kadashman-Enlil sent his daughter as a
bride to Pharaoh Amenhotep the Third.</s></p>
<p t="182850" d="4479" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This people who came down from
the Zagros now sat at the same
table as the most powerful kings</s></p>
<p t="187369" d="1100" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">in the world.</s></p>
<p t="188560" d="2039" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The trade network of Kassite</s></p>
<p t="190640" d="4680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Babylon stretched from the lapis lazuli
mines of Afghanistan to Dilmun in the</s></p>
<p t="195359" d="920" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Persian Gulf.</s></p>
<p t="196320" d="3980" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Dilmun, today's Bahrain, was
governed by a Kassite governor.</s></p>
<p t="204999" d="3560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites became the guardians
of Mesopotamian civilization.</s></p>
<p t="208599" d="4880" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">King Kurigalzu founded the city of
Dur-Kurigalzu, bearing his own name, near</s></p>
<p t="213519" d="1000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">today's Baghdad.</s></p>
<p t="214559" d="2879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The ziggurat of this
city still stands today.</s></p>
<p t="217479" d="4440" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites rebuilt the temples of
ancient cities such as Nippur and</s></p>
<p t="221959" d="560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Uruk.</s></p>
<p t="222559" d="4759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The standard texts of great works
such as the Epic of Gilgamesh
were compiled in this period.</s></p>
<p t="227359" d="3240" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites also brought
a new tradition to</s></p>
<p t="230640" d="2199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Mesopotamia: boundary
stones called kudurru.</s></p>
<p t="232879" d="3240" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Kings had the lands they granted
inscribed on these stones,</s></p>
<p t="236159" d="3013" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">with the symbols of the
gods carved upon them.</s></p>
<p t="239212" d="2987" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites honored the
gods of Babylon, but they</s></p>
<p t="242239" d="2240" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">did not forget their own mountain gods.</s></p>
<p t="244519" d="4460" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Shuqamuna and Shumaliya were the
guardian gods of the Zagros mountains.</s></p>
<p t="249069" d="4200" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Thousands of tablets from the Kassite period
were found in the excavations at Nippur.</s></p>
<p t="253309" d="4559" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">These tablets describe daily life,
from tax records to the care of horses.</s></p>
<p t="257909" d="3140" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Kassite craftsmen also mastered
the art of glassmaking.</s></p>
<p t="265749" d="4839" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the twelfth century BC, Elam, the
neighbor to the east, went on the attack.</s></p>
<p t="270629" d="5079" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Elamite king Shutruk-Nahhunte
sacked Babylon and carried the
famous stele inscribed with the Laws</s></p>
<p t="275749" d="2719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of Hammurabi off to the city of Susa.</s></p>
<p t="278509" d="3680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">That stele is displayed today
in the Louvre Museum in Paris.</s></p>
<p t="282229" d="5359" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Around 1155 BC, the last Kassite
king, Enlil-nadin-ahi, lost his</s></p>
<p t="287629" d="759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">throne.</s></p>
<p t="288429" d="2900" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassite dynasty in
Babylon came to an end.</s></p>
<p t="296049" d="1839" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But the Kassites did not disappear.</s></p>
<p t="297929" d="4199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Having lost the throne of
Babylon, they withdrew to their
homeland, the Zagros mountains.</s></p>
<p t="302169" d="4359" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">For centuries, Assyrian kings led
campaigns into the Kassite land.</s></p>
<p t="306569" d="4719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Assyrian king Sennacherib
recorded in his inscriptions
that in 702 BC he marched into</s></p>
<p t="311329" d="2000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the mountain homeland of the Kassites.</s></p>
<p t="313369" d="4639" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">A thousand years later, Greek
historians wrote of the same
people under the name Kossaioi.</s></p>
<p t="318049" d="3239" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Strabo writes that the
Kossaioi were skilled archers.</s></p>
<p t="321329" d="4040" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Arrian tells how Alexander the
Great led a winter campaign against</s></p>
<p t="325409" d="719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">them.</s></p>
<p t="326169" d="4159" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Even the Persian kings paid the
Kossaioi a toll when crossing the</s></p>
<p t="330369" d="2199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">mountain road between Susa and Ecbatana.</s></p>
<p t="332609" d="3420" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Once again, the Zagros mountains
had protected a people.</s></p>
<p t="336119" d="4919" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Today, the Kurdish and Lur tribes living
in the same valleys of the Zagros carry</s></p>
<p t="341079" d="3180" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">on this mountain life that has
lasted for thousands of years.</s></p>
<p t="349039" d="2599" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kassites were not
alone in these lands.</s></p>
<p t="351679" d="2439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Gutians, Lullubi, Hurrians, Mitanni</s></p>
<p t="354159" d="4412" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and Medes also lived to the
north and east of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="358612" d="4307" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">According to many historians,
these peoples are the ancient
layers that formed the Kurds.</s></p>
<p t="362960" d="3479" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Foremost among those who hold
this view are foreign scholars.</s></p>
<p t="366479" d="2519" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The American Assyriologist
Ephraim Speiser, in</s></p>
<p t="369039" d="5159" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">his work Mesopotamian Origins,
counts the Kassites, together
with the Gutians and the Lullubi,</s></p>
<p t="374239" d="2439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">among the native peoples of the Zagros.</s></p>
<p t="376719" d="4759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Arshak Safrastian, in his book Kurds and
Kurdistan, examines the peoples of the Zagros</s></p>
<p t="381520" d="2092" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">as the ancestors of the Kurds.</s></p>
<p t="383652" d="4546" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Mehrdad Izady presents the Kassites as
one of the most important layers forming</s></p>
<p t="388239" d="4199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Kurdish people, and looks for the
traces of Kassite mountain traditions in</s></p>
<p t="392479" d="1106" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Kurdish society today.</s></p>
<p t="393626" d="4332" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kurdish historian Muhammad Amin
Zaki Beg also counts the Kassites
among the ancient ancestors</s></p>
<p t="397999" d="1079" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of the Kurds.</s></p>
<p t="399119" d="2067" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Of course, there are
opposing views as well.</s></p>
<p t="401227" d="4092" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The main reason is that very few documents
in the Kassite language have survived.</s></p>
<p t="405359" d="3719" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">All we have is a bilingual
glossary and the names of kings.</s></p>
<p t="409119" d="4492" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Yet geography, mountain life and a
continuity of thousands of years make this</s></p>
<p t="413652" d="1000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">bond strong.</s></p>
<p t="414979" d="2079" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">A people who came down
from the Zagros mountains</s></p>
<p t="417099" d="3560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">ruled Babylon, the most famous city
in the world, for four hundred years.</s></p>
<p t="420700" d="2039" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">They sat at the same table as the</s></p>
<p t="422780" d="4439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">pharaohs, protected the civilization
of Mesopotamia, and in the end
returned once more to their mountains.</s></p>
<p t="427259" d="4439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The story of the Kassites is one of
the oldest pages of Kurdish history.</s></p>
<p t="431739" d="4279" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">If you enjoyed this video, don't forget
to subscribe and share your thoughts in</s></p>
<p t="436059" d="1000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the comments.</s></p>
</body>
</timedtext>
